<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Galway</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Galway is a city on the west coast of Ireland, at the mouth of the River Corrib on Galway Bay, and gives its name to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-galway">County Galway</a>. Founded as an Anglo-Norman town by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-burgh">de Burgh family</a> in the 13th century, it was governed by merchant families known as the Tribes of Galway and traded widely with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The surrounding county takes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connemara">Connemara</a> and the old lordships of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oflaherty">O'Flahertys</a> and the Clanricarde Burkes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galway.jpg" alt="Galway" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Galway_cathedral.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Matthew <b>Galway</b> writes that the party did not de-Maoize but reassessed Mao’s legacy, stressing his positive contributions while acknowledging his mistakes.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/maoism">Maoism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its second strain is identical to the Irish tune &quot;All the way to <b>Galway</b>&quot;, from which it may have come.</blockquote><a href="/songs/yankee-doodle">Yankee Doodle</a></li><li><blockquote>The Auteini, or Autini, are listed in Ptolemy's Geography of about 150 on the west coast of Ireland, between the Nagnatae to the north and the Gangani (Ireland) to the south, roughly in modern <b>Galway</b> or Mayo.</blockquote><a href="/state/auteini">Auteini</a></li><li><blockquote>The southern branch, led by Ulick de Burgh, adopted the Gaelic title Mac William Uachtar, the Upper Mac William, and ruled south <b>Galway</b> from Loughrea.</blockquote><a href="/state/clanricarde">Clanricarde</a></li><li><blockquote>They were frequently at war with the merchant town of <b>Galway</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/iar-connacht">Iar Connacht</a></li></ul></section>
